title: Following a task
tags: Tasks
kind: guide
summary: See what the daemon is doing — mf task, the GUI's task bar and the repos panel — and why your request is waiting.

!! From the command line

```
mf task                 # the active repository's tasks (mf task list)
mf task list --all      # every loaded repository's
mf task show <id>       # one task
```

Each row gives the task's id, kind, status, phase and progress; `--json` prints
the raw objects ([[Task endpoints]]). All kinds are listed, watcher flushes
included.

The commands that start long work follow their own task and draw its progress on
the terminal (a bar or a spinner, only when stderr is a terminal): `mf reconcile`,
`mf duplicate scan`, `mf repo load`. Their `--no-wait` prints the task id and
returns; `mf task show <id>` then tells you how it went.

!! In the GUI

The ''task bar'', below the panels, lists every pending or running task of every
loaded repository — a bar when the task knows its amount of work, a spinner
labelled with its phase otherwise — and hides when there is none. A task appears
only once it has run for a second: a watcher flush lasts milliseconds and fires
whenever a watched file is touched, and showing it would make the panels jump
for work nobody asked to see (what a flush did is reported in the `message`
panel instead). Since it polls the daemon, it shows any task, not only the GUI's
own, and reopening the GUI mid-reconcile resumes showing it ([[Status bar and task bar]]).

The [[repos panel]] lists each repository's active tasks under it, with a
''Stop'' button for those that can be stopped ([[Cancelling a task]]).

!! Why is my request waiting?

A repository runs one task at a time ([[Task concurrency]]): a query that arrives
during a reconcile shows as `pending` until the reconcile is done. Listing the
tasks tells you what it is waiting for.
